<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juba I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 85–<a href="/years/46-bce">46 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Juba I of Numidia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juba I was king of Numidia from about 60 to 46 BCE. In Caesar's civil war he sided with the Pompeians, destroying the army of Caesar's legate Curio on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a> river in 49 BCE, but after the Pompeian defeat at Thapsus in 46 BCE he took his own life. Caesar annexed much of his kingdom as the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa-nova">Africa Nova</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juba-i.jpg" alt="Juba I" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juba_i.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juba I was an Amazigh prince of Numidia, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, born at Hippo, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Annaba</a>, and son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiempsal-ii">Hiempsal II</a>. In 81 BCE Hiempsal had been driven from his throne, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to restore him. For that reason Hiempsal and later Juba were allies of Pompey. The alliance was strengthened when Juba visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, defending a client against Juba's father, insulted him by pulling his beard, and again in 50 BCE when the tribune Gaius Scribonius Curio openly proposed that Numidia should be sold privately.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In August 49 BCE Caesar sent Curio to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from the Republicans. Curio held the governor Publius Attius Varus in low esteem and took fewer legions than he had been given. At the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a> he routed Varus's army with an uphill attack, then acted on faulty intelligence and attacked what he took for a detachment of Juba's army. The bulk of the king's forces were there, and Juba's commander Saburra ambushed and virtually destroyed the Roman force. Curio died in the fighting on a hilltop, few escaped by ship, and Juba took several senators captive back to Numidia for display and execution.</p><p>When Caesar came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Juba first planned to join Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius Scipio Nasica, but his kingdom was invaded from the west by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-ii">Bocchus II</a>, an ally of Caesar, and the adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-sittius">Publius Sittius</a>, and he marched home. Scipio then appealed for help, and Juba joined him with three legions, about 15,000 light infantry, 1,000 cavalry and 30 elephants for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus">Battle of Thapsus</a>, camping apart from Scipio's main lines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Seeing Scipio's defeat to be certain, Juba took no part in the battle and fled with his 30,000 men. He and the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-petreius">Marcus Petreius</a> found their retreat cut off and made a suicide pact, fighting one against the other so that one would die honourably. Accounts of their deaths differ; in the most likely version one killed the other, and the survivor, failing to stab himself, had a slave kill him. Juba was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a>, later king of Numidia and then Mauretania, whose wives were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii">Cleopatra Selene II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaphyra">Glaphyra</a>, and the grandfather of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-of-mauretania">Ptolemy of Mauretania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juba I of Numidia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his victory over the Pompeians and <b>Juba I</b> at the Battle of Thapsus in 46 BCE, Julius Caesar annexed much of Numidia as a second province, Africa Nova, so that the old province became Africa Vetus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 49 BCE <b>Juba I</b> destroyed the army of Gaius Scribonius Curio at the Battle of the Bagradas River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Thapsus, in present-day Tunisia, Julius Caesar defeated the Pompeian forces of Metellus Scipio and King <b>Juba I of Numidia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-6">6 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In Caesar's Civil War, the Pompeians under Publius Attius Varus and King <b>Juba I of Numidia</b> routed the army of Caesar's general Gaius Scribonius Curio near the Bagradas River, in present-day Tunisia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Having backed <b>Juba I</b> in Caesar's civil war, it was fined three million pounds of olive oil a year in 46 BCE and absorbed into Roman Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a></li><li><blockquote>Numidia came under Rome after the defeat of <b>Juba I</b> in 46 BCE and was long administered as part of Africa Proconsularis, although the commander of the Third Augustan Legion effectively governed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-numidia">Roman Numidia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in Zama Regia, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.88,7.75&amp;year=-60" aria-label="Hippo Regius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4744 835 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juba I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 1105h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 1105h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.88,7.75&amp;year=-60">What was here in 60 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
